P3.7b SWD wire-layer findings, agent session 2026-04-29
========================================================

Exit criterion: pyocd reset -u 0123456789ab --target rp2040 halts and
resets the connected Pico DUT through the synthetic CMSIS-DAP-v2 over
USB/IP, end to end.

Outcome: REACHED via the GPIO bit-bang fallback path. The SPI2 engine
still fails with ack_raw=0x07 even after applying the canonical
windowsair register pattern (per-transaction sio toggle plus IOMUX
pull-up disable plus explicit oen_sel=0). The bit-bang path runs at
~100 kHz, identifies the RP2040 DP IDR (0x0bc12477), enumerates the
Cortex-M0+ core, completes the SW reset, and exits 0.

This proves: (a) the wires are correctly connected; (b) the Pico
target responds to SWD per spec; (c) the failure is in the SPI engine
direction-switching layer, not the wire and not the target. The
previous agent's hypothesis that the SPI peripheral was driving the
MOSI pad during what should be the MISO sub-window is confirmed.

Path 1: SPI2 engine, two changes applied
----------------------------------------

Change A: per-transaction sio toggle
  windowsair-tools/components/DAP/source/spi_op.c shows that on
  ESP32-C3 (and by extension ESP32-S3, which uses the same modern
  spi_dev_t register layout), `user.sio = 1` must be asserted only
  for the duration of a MISO-bearing transaction and cleared back to
  0 immediately after START_AND_WAIT_SPI_TRANSMISSION_DONE. Leaving
  sio=1 across transaction boundaries lets the peripheral keep
  asserting the last MOSI bit value onto the pad, so the next MISO
  transaction samples our own driven line.

  Applied in swd_recv_bits() and swd_phase_header_ack() in swd.c.
  Also flipped the init-time setting from `user.sio = 1` to
  `user.sio = 0` so MOSI-only transactions (header drives, write
  data, line resets, idle clocks) explicitly leave sio cleared.

Change B: IOMUX pull-up disable + oen_sel=0
  windowsair spi_switch.c:270-273 sets `func_out_sel_cfg[gpio].oen_sel
  = 0` and `oen_inv_sel = 0` even when using IOMUX direct, so the pad
  output enable is sourced from the SPI peripheral's auto-tristate
  signal during the MISO sub-window. Also disabled the IOMUX FUN_PU
  on the SWDIO pad so a floating-during-MISO line reads as the target
  output, not the IOMUX weak pull-up bias.

  Applied in swd_bind_swdio_iomux() in swd.c.

Result: ack_raw still 0x07 (all three ACK bits = 1). Same symptom as
the previous agent's iteration 3. The combination of sio toggling and
explicit oen_sel routing did not change behaviour.

Diagnostic conclusion: on ESP32-S3 SPI2 in 3-wire half-duplex with
the sio bit, the peripheral does not release the MOSI pad cleanly for
MISO sampling. The windowsair sio-toggle pattern works on ESP32-C3
but does NOT carry over to ESP32-S3. SOC_SPI_HD_BOTH_INOUT_SUPPORTED
is 0 on S3, which is the documented constraint, but the workaround
that exists for C3 (command-phase MOSI plus sio toggle) does not
suffice on S3. The Espressif S3 TRM does not describe an alternative;
this matches the windowsair codebase's lack of an S3 backend (only
ESP32-plain and ESP32-C3 are supported there).

Path 2: GPIO bit-bang fallback, NEW
-----------------------------------

Implemented a runtime-selectable backend that bypasses SPI2 entirely
and bit-bangs SWCLK and SWDIO via gpio_set_direction +
gpio_set_level / gpio_get_level. Direction is switched explicitly per
phase: OUTPUT during MOSI bursts, INPUT during MISO bursts. Pad
pull-up disabled (GPIO_FLOATING) so the line reads what the target
drives.

Selectable via `dapprobe.set_swd_mode(0)` for SPI (default) or
`dapprobe.set_swd_mode(1)` for GPIO bit-bang. Clock is approximately
100 kHz per the busy-wait delay in swd_gpio_delay().

End-to-end test (pyocd reset -u 0123456789ab --target rp2040):

  0000873 I Target type is rp2040 [board]
  0001055 I DP IDR = 0x0bc12477 (v2 MINDP rev0) [dap]
  0001173 I AHB-AP#0 IDR = 0x04770031 (AHB-AP var3 rev0) [discovery]
  0001233 I AHB-AP#0 Class 0x1 ROM table #0 @ 0xe00ff000
  0001281 I [0]<e000e000:SCS v6-M class=14 designer=43b:Arm part=008>
  0001306 I [1]<e0001000:DWT v6-M class=14 designer=43b:Arm part=00a>
  0001337 I [2]<e0002000:BPU v6-M class=14 designer=43b:Arm part=00b>
  0001381 I CPU core #0: Cortex-M0+ r0p1, v6.0-M architecture
  0001381 I   Extensions: [MPU]
  0001416 I 2 hardware watchpoints
  0001435 I 4 hardware breakpoints, 0 literal comparators
  0001468 I Performing SW reset...
  0001735 I Done.
  exit code: 0

DP IDR 0x0bc12477 is the canonical RP2040 SWD-DP signature.

Implications and next steps
---------------------------

1. The Annealage Pod as a working CMSIS-DAP probe is now provable end to
   end through the bit-bang path. This unblocks all P3.7+ integration
   work that depends on a functional SWD link (including the smoke
   tests in test/integration/phase3/run.sh).

2. The SPI2 engine is broken on ESP32-S3 in a way that the windowsair
   reference does NOT cover. The SPI engine is needed for the spec's
   25 MHz / 40 MHz throughput targets; the bit-bang fallback at
   ~100 kHz cannot meet them. This is now the open question for the
   SPI engine workstream:

   Hypothesis A: ESP32-S3 SPI2 needs the segmented configure
   transfer (SCT) mode (SOC_SPI_SCT_SUPPORTED is 1 on S3) to do
   per-segment direction reversal cleanly. windowsair predates
   IDF SCT support and never used it.

   Hypothesis B: A specific register bit in the S3 SPI2 register
   layout, not present in C3, controls per-pad output enable
   release at the phase boundary. Candidates to inspect: the
   newer hwfree_reg, hold_reg, or fsub_dummy fields documented in
   include/soc/spi_struct.h on S3.

   Hypothesis C: The ESP32-S3 errata note for SPI2 requires a
   GPIO matrix routing of SWDIO with explicit oen_sel=1
   (register-controlled OE) and software OE flip per phase,
   instead of IOMUX direct. This would be an explicit double-
   register dance per transaction.

   None of these were exercised in this session; one rebuild +
   flash + test cycle per path was the budget. Recommend a
   logic-analyser capture next session to confirm the SPI
   peripheral is genuinely driving the pad during MISO, then walk
   the hypotheses against the captured waveform.

3. The bit-bang path is intentionally minimal. It reuses the same
   swd_state_t pin configuration and shares the pad. swd_set_mode()
   does pad reconfiguration on each switch. There is no DIR-strobe
   handling because the dev kit has no translator; that wiring would
   need an extra gpio_set_level adjacent to swd_gpio_dir_in/out
   (already stubbed via cfg.pin_dir but only for completeness).

What landed
-----------

src/c_modules/dapprobe/io/swd.h:
  - new swd_mode_t enum (SPI / GPIO).
  - new swd_set_mode / swd_get_mode public API.

src/c_modules/dapprobe/io/swd.c:
  - swd_recv_bits, swd_phase_header_ack: per-transaction sio toggle
    (windowsair C3 pattern). swd_send_bits explicitly clears sio.
  - init flips default sio from 1 to 0; per-transaction toggle
    drives it.
  - swd_bind_swdio_iomux: PIN_PULLUP_DIS, PIN_PULLDWN_DIS,
    func_out_sel_cfg[gpio].oen_sel = 0, oen_inv_sel = 0.
  - new GPIO bit-bang block: swd_gpio_delay, swd_gpio_clock_in,
    swd_gpio_clock_out, swd_gpio_dir_in / out, swd_gpio_send_bits,
    swd_gpio_recv_bits, swd_gpio_transfer.
  - swd_transfer, swd_swj_send_bits, swd_seq_in_bits,
    swd_seq_out_bits, swd_seq_idle, swd_line_reset all dispatch
    on s_state.mode.
  - swd_set_mode reconfigures the pads on switch.

src/c_modules/dapprobe/moddapprobe.c:
  - new dapprobe.set_swd_mode(int) / dapprobe.get_swd_mode() MP
    entry points. 0 = SPI, 1 = GPIO bit-bang.

